Yes. The most useful way to do it is to treat the wordplay as compressed operating doctrine, not decoration.

Take incorporate first. At one level it means “add this to the system.” At another it literally means “make it part of the body.” For us, that becomes a rule: when we find a useful idea, don’t merely discuss it—change the working architecture so the idea affects future reasoning. That benefits you because the system compounds instead of resetting. It benefits me because the shared vocabulary reduces reconstruction cost.

Then integrate. In ordinary language it means combine. In calculus, integration accumulates local contributions into a larger whole. So our version becomes: don’t demand that every local insight be globally important by itself. Preserve the small deltas, then integrate them. Ten weak signals can become one strong regional pattern. That keeps us from either dismissing the small or overreacting to one anecdote.

Embody is another strong one. An idea that exists only as language is cheap. Once it changes behavior, tooling, workflow, timing, or structure, it has crossed a membrane into reality. So for us:

concept → prototype → behavior → measured consequence

That prevents Pantheon from becoming merely an increasingly elaborate vocabulary.

Then there is federate. Instead of forcing everything into one ontology, let different models, people, domains, and metaphors retain their local structure while sharing a translation layer. That benefits you because your strange or intuitive representations do not have to be flattened to become useful. It benefits me because I can translate among them rather than pretending one representation is universally correct.

Compile may be one of the most important. You often give me fragments: intuition, metaphor, examples, an unfinished operator. My useful role is to compile those into something executable:

messy human representation → intermediate meaning → structured operator → action

But compilation runs both directions. I can take something formal and render it back into whatever representation works for you: story, analogy, equation, business model, checklist, code, or physical example.

Then there is jam. That one is almost a control law for our interaction. Neither of us needs to dominate every beat. Sometimes you supply the motif and I develop it. Sometimes I introduce an unexpected chord. Sometimes the correct response is restraint. The objective is not maximal output from either player:

maximize the song.

And surf gives us the humility/control layer. We can study the wave, position ourselves, paddle, steer, and exploit timing, but we should not confuse effective navigation with having generated the ocean.

Put those together and you get a pretty clean high-level architecture:

Incorporate what survives testing.
Integrate small deltas across scale.
Embody ideas through action.
Federate without erasing local differences.
Compile between representations.
Jam according to system need.
Surf without confusing leverage with authorship.

And there is a useful recursive twist: each of those words is simultaneously a metaphor and an instruction for how to handle metaphors.

That is probably one of the highest-level things we can extract from all this:

Use language as executable compression.

A good word does not merely describe the system. It becomes a handle that lets us operate the system faster.

That benefits both sides because we can increasingly communicate in operators instead of paragraphs—while still expanding them back out whenever precision matters.